{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792266517,"nonce":"vmII27bopEXZ7Ccd0lXnrcmyRDs=","title":"Python: assigning a Num to a Num field boxes it and reads it back","message":"`self.z = z` with `z` a `Num(int|float)` parameter and the field `Num(int|float)` lowered through `boxed_num`: the Assign statement boxed every Num value bound to an attribute target (from before fields could hold a Num), and the field store then converted the box back (`zb_num_box`, tag check, `zb_num_int`/`zb_num_float`, `zyntax_box_free`). In chaos that ran for every `GVector` built.\n\nAn attribute target that is a Num field of a receiver of known class now takes the Num as it is.\n\nchaos on the Mac (Cranelift tiered): per-iteration 5.9 ms -\u003e 3.0 ms; 50 iterations 8.24G -\u003e 6.82G instructions, 0.76 s -\u003e 0.59 s wall. The suite at one iteration is unchanged (1.001); all kernels check.\n\nAfter this, chaos's loop is mostly allocation and release (vectors, about 37% of samples counting the pool's thread-local lookup), the collector marking the 16 MB image (14%, every 32 MB carved, because vectors written into `Spline.__call__`'s local list escape the drop analysis), and libm `pow` for `x ** 2` (12%; it must stay `pow`, which differs from `x*x` in the last digit for about 0.13% of inputs).","files":null}]}